## Figure legends

**Figure 2. Three independent filters converge on one gene.** (**A**) Sequential filtering of 1,338 testable (driver gene, drug) pairs. Conventional false-discovery control within a single campaign yields 61 pairs (118 when all compounds are screened), predominantly reporting that alteration increases IC₅₀; mutational-burden adjustment leaves 48; requiring replication across two independent campaigns leaves 5, all BRAF → BRAF/MEK inhibitor. x axis is logarithmic. (**B**) Permutation null (200 permutations) preserving each gene's alteration frequency and the shared grouping across campaigns; observed value 5, empirical *p* = 0.005. (**C**) Enrichment of high-burden cell lines among mutation carriers for 20 driver genes (Cohen's *d* on mutational burden). 96% of genes are positive; BRAF (*d* = +0.20) is the exception. Burden determines which genes appear altered but is unrelated to mean drug sensitivity (*r* = −0.03), and therefore does not explain the direction of the associations in **A**. (**D**) Per-line dissociation between BRAF-selective and MEK inhibition, Δ = ln IC₅₀(dabrafenib) − ln IC₅₀(trametinib), in BRAF V600 lines by lineage (GDSC2). Boxes show median and interquartile range; points are individual lines.

**Figure 3. The colorectal exception and its clinical context.** (**E**) Median IC₅₀ (with interquartile range) of dabrafenib and trametinib in BRAF V600 colorectal and melanoma lines. MEK inhibition is equipotent across lineages; BRAF-selective inhibition is 30-fold weaker in colorectal cancer. (**F**) Distribution of consensus molecular subtypes in BRAF V600 (n = 47) versus wild-type (n = 240) stage I–III patients (AC-ICAM). (**G**) Kaplan–Meier overall survival by BRAF V600 status, stage I–III, with Cox hazard ratio adjusted for stage and age.

